== 9. Of the Duties of Married Persons

Thus far we have proceeded in the doctrine of marriage; and now we come to the duties which married persons are to perform to each other.

These are chiefly two: cohabitation and communion.

#emph[Cohabitation is their quiet and comforting dwelling together in one place, for the better performance of mutual duties.] 1 Cor 7:10: “To the married I give this command (not I, but the Lord): A wife must not separate from her husband.” v. 12: “To the rest I say this (I, not the Lord): If a brother has an unbelieving wife and she is willing to live with him, he must not leave her.” v. 13: “And if a woman has an unbelieving husband and he is willing to live with her, she must not leave him.” 1 Pet 3:7: “Husbands, in the same way, live with your#super[+] wives with consideration, giving honor to the woman as the weaker vessel and as fellow heirs of the gracious gift of life, so that your prayers will not be hindered.”

This duty must be kept especially in the first year of marriage. Deut 24:5: “If a man is newly married, he must not be sent to war or be pressed into any duty. For one year he is free to stay at home and bring joy to the wife he has married.” And the ground of this commandment, no doubt, is that they might learn to know one another’s dispositions, and might form a settled affection for one another, which afterward might not be changed on any occasion.

Yet they may be absent from each other in two cases. First, by mutual consent for a time, to carry out some business that the family requires. Prov 7:19: “For my husband is not at home; he has gone on a long journey … and will not return till the moon is full.” Secondly, such absence is allowed when some great and weighty affairs, either in the Church or the commonwealth, are in hand. 2 Sam 11:9, 10: “But Uriah slept at the door of the palace with all his master’s servants; he did not go down to his house.” v. 11: Uriah answered David, “The ark and Israel and Judah are dwelling in tents, and my master Joab and his soldiers are camped in the open field. How can I go to my house to eat and drink and sleep with my wife? As surely as you live, and as your soul lives, I will not do such a thing!”

From this arises a question: What if, after the marriage is completed, a contagious disease comes upon one of the parties that cannot be cured, but is so loathsome that the other, healthy party cannot endure living together at all?

#emph[Answer.] Both the marriage itself and the duty of dwelling together ought to continue firm and sure notwithstanding. Yet the sound party neither may nor ought, in good conscience, to desire union with the other by which he or she may bring infection upon themselves, and so hurt their children and endanger the commonwealth.

But if either of the parties does not have the gift of self-control and cannot abstain, they must seek it from God in earnest prayer, and he will give it to them.

For God has called a man or woman to continency where they are necessarily hindered from the use of the ordinary and lawful remedy of the contract.

The contrary of cohabitation is desertion.

Desertion is when one of the married couple, from a wilful and obstinate mind, and on his or her own initiative, departs from the other without a just and necessary cause.

On this point various cases are explained.

I. Case. Suppose that a husband who is an unbeliever, or a heretic in the fundamentals, of his own accord and out of hatred of the true religion, completely forsakes his believing wife and refuses to live with her any longer. What is to be done?

#emph[Answer.] All good means must first be used to bring the infected party to repentance. And when none succeed, but the case remains desperate, then the marriage is dissolved on his part, and the believing wife is free to marry another. So says the apostle, 1 Cor 7:15: “But if the unbeliever leaves, let him go. The believing brother or sister is not bound in such cases. God has called us to live in peace.” In these words observe a twofold reason. I. He who is at liberty may marry another. Now the believing party who is forsaken is at liberty, because he is not bound in such cases. Therefore he may marry another. II. God has called us to peace, says Paul; and this peace cannot be kept if the forsaken believing party cannot live continently unless he marries. Ambrose on the place says#footnote[Ambros. in 1 Cor 7:15.] “that marriage cannot stand firm which is separated from the worship and service of God; and therefore it is no sin in the party who is forsaken for God’s cause to be married to another; and the unbeliever who departs sins both against God and against the law of wedlock, because he refuses to remain in that state on account of religion. Therefore he breaks the covenant of marriage, and the other is not bound in this case to keep faith with him.” Lombard, the Master of the Sentences,#footnote[Libr. 4. dist. 39.] says “that the believing party in this case is not bound to follow the unbeliever when he departs, but while he lives may marry another.” Augustine#footnote[Lib. de Fide & operib. cap. 16.] affirms “that even the wife who is joined to her husband in lawful union, if she will not abide with her Christian husband because he is a Christian, may be forsaken and left without sin.” And it is far better that the covenant which man and wife have made with each other should be dissolved than the covenant which man has made with God. The people of Israel, being in affliction, were compelled to break the former, made with foreign women, so that they might keep the latter (Ezra 10:11, 19).

It is alleged that the unbelieving party may perhaps repent, and then to make a dissolution is in vain. But I say that it is just as likely that he will never repent, and therefore there is cause for the knot to be dissolved.

Again, some say that it is then lawful to make a divorce outside the case of adultery. #emph[Answer.] The believing party who is forsaken is not the cause of the divorce, but suffers the separation wrongfully made by the unbeliever. And Christ, where he mentions the case of adultery (Matt 19), speaks of an equal marriage, whereas Paul speaks of an unequal one.

II. Case. What if a desertion happens between two married persons who are both believers?

#emph[Answer.] The faulty person, who is the cause of this desertion, is to be compelled by the course of civil and ecclesiastical censure to perform his or her duty. If upon this proceeding he remains obstinate and perverse in will, the other must wait in patience and earnest prayer to God, until his mind may be changed and he is made to relent by the order of the magistrate. But one of them may, through a just occasion of fear, be compelled to depart from the other, and be unable to return without evident danger of life. In this case the one who departed is not bound to return; but the delinquent party is to remain alone until he or she is instructed and made willing to do his or her duty. And in the meantime the innocent party must be resolved that God has called him or her to single life.

Again, suppose that one is resolutely unwilling to live with the other, and on that account flees without any fault of the other. If the matter, after a long time, is sufficiently known beforehand, and all possible means have been used to reclaim the guilty person; and if, when called, he does not appear in person before the judge to give a reason for his act: then, after public and solemn declaration has been made, the minister may, on such desertion, pronounce the marriage dissolved. For he who out of malice flees from his mate is to be held on the same footing as an unbeliever who departs out of hatred of religion and the service of God (1 Tim 5:8).

However, if the place to which he has fled is unknown, and on his flight some fault is found in the plaintiff, she is not to be heard, or set at liberty, by the judge in this case, though she pleads lack of the gift of self-control.

But suppose he who fled comes back unexpectedly and claims his spouse. Then, if the former course has not been taken, nor judgment given against him, they may be reconciled and come together again. If it has, and matters are concluded, he is not to be heard on his return, but rather to be severely punished.

Like #emph[desertion] is the malicious and spiteful dealing of married persons with one another.

#emph[Malicious dealing] is when, living together, they demand intolerable conditions of each other; and when the one does not regard or relieve the other, being in danger or extremity, as is fitting. For this is as much as to betray one another’s estate and life to their utter enemies.

Here it may be asked what a believer should do who is in certain and imminent danger, either of loss of life or of breach of conscience, if they both remain together.

#emph[Answer.] I. This certain danger has its origin either from a stranger or from one of the parties. If from a stranger, then the husband either takes on himself the defense of his believing wife, or he does not. If he does, then she ought to remain with him. If not, she may depart and provide for her own safety. II. Again, if the husband threatens harm, the believing wife may flee in this case; and it is all one as if the unbelieving man departed. For to depart from someone and to drive someone away by threats are equivalent.

Nor should it seem strange to anyone that the believer is allowed to depart in such a case. For a husband who is a Christian is married in two ways: first to Christ, and secondly to his wife. The former marriage is made in baptism, and is a holier union than the latter. Therefore, when these two cannot stand together, but one of them must necessarily be dissolved, the latter must be left rather than the former. Again, if the believer remained with the unbeliever, she might perhaps at times be pressed, in a case of danger, through infirmity, to deny Christ and make shipwreck of faith and good conscience, which may in no way be done by either party. And therefore separation is to be made in this society, rather than that the union with Christ should not stand firm and continue.

It is alleged that if this is so, then the believing wife forsakes the unbelieving husband, which she may not do.

#emph[Answer.] She does not forsake him finally, but leaves him for a time. Again, the desertion is not made by the person who gives way for the time, but by him in whom the cause of the desertion lies; just as he is not to be called a schismatic who separates himself, but he in whom the cause of the schism lies.

Again, it is objected that he who flees leaves his calling, and every man must remain in his calling, according to the rule of the apostle (1 Cor 7:20).

#emph[Answer.] There is a double calling: the general, which consists in the worship of God; the particular, as the calling of marriage or of single life. When these two callings cannot stand together, the latter must give way to the former. Luke 14:26: “If anyone comes to Me and does not hate his father and mother and wife and children and brothers and sisters—yes, even his own life—he cannot be My disciple.”

III. Case. When the husband is perpetually absent from the wife, what is to be done?

#emph[Answer.] If he is absent because he is in captivity, or out of malice, or fear, or any such cause, the wife must wait in expectation of his return until she has notice of his death, either by sufficient testimony or by evident likelihoods. Now, in the lack of testimonies and conjectures in this matter, if the forsaken party cannot determine with certainty that the absence was made with an evil mind, some have thought that she ought to wait for his return for the space of four years; others five; some seven, some ten years. After that time she is free, and may marry another man. And if the party who was probably thought to be dead does perhaps return after a long time, the latter marriage, undertaken in necessary ignorance of such an event, may be dissolved; and it will not be imputed to either party, since it happened not by their fault, but only by accident.

Now if the question is of wilful and deliberate absence, the same is to be determined concerning it as has been said before on the point of desertion.
